WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.070
Vsauce!

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:07.919
Kevin here -- with a PERFECT explanation for
a 56% reduction in violent crime between 1992

00:00:07.919 --> 00:00:09.349
and 2002.

00:00:09.349 --> 00:00:14.920
A study of Uniform Crime Reporting data found
that removing lead from gasoline over a decade

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:20.860
before reduced lead's detrimental effects
on children's developing brains -- so when

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:23.600
they grew up, they committed less crime.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:24.880
Makes sense, right?

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:30.680
WRONG -- using the Bureau of Justice Statistics
National Crime Victimization Survey data showed

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:33.000
that leaded gasoline had...

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.200
no effect at all on violent crime rates.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.000
It wasn’t 56%, it was ZERO percent.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:46.770
Two data sets led to 2 completely OPPOSITE
conclusions -- because measuring social phenomena

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.250
is hard.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:54.780
And it's actually a really new thing -- 200
years ago, no one even measured crime rates.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.160
Now we measure everything.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:01.890
The problem is that most of our measurements…

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.570
are wrong.

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:13.570
We’ve been counting people for a while -- China
had censuses over 4,000 years ago, the Romans

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:19.430
conducted censuses for taxation and military
conscription -- “censere” in Latin literally

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:25.160
means “to tax” -- and William the Conqueror
ordered The Domesday Book to be compiled in

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:30.950
1086 -- but even then it was just counting
people, property, and farm animals.

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:37.470
There was nothing about people’s lives -- let
alone keeping track of what they did to harm

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:38.750
others.

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:44.439
Democracy had matured by the 5th century BC
in Athens, but demography didn’t exist until

00:01:44.439 --> 00:01:46.250
the 1600’s.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:51.820
English haberdashier John Graunt analyzed
the weekly “Bills of Mortality” -- official

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:59.369
lists of births, deaths, and causes of death
-- to better understand life in the 1660’s.

00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:04.829
170 years later, Andre-Michel Guerry changed
everything.

00:02:04.829 --> 00:02:11.819
His 1832 presentation to the French Academy
of Sciences detailed the “moral statistics

00:02:11.819 --> 00:02:18.420
of France” -- assaults, murders, suicides,
Guerry tracked them all, revealing that their

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:24.270
rates differed throughout France and by age,
sex, race and the other general demographics

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.129
we still use.

00:02:26.129 --> 00:02:32.790
There were some odd findings -- like literacy
rates weren’t correlated with crimes against

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:40.069
people, while higher literacy rates were correlated
with crimes against property.

00:02:40.069 --> 00:02:43.680
Apparently, reading makes you break things.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.930
And Math had finally collided with morality.

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:50.599
The social statistics era boomed.

00:02:50.599 --> 00:02:53.700
Some people wanted to lock up more criminals.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:59.160
Others wanted to spend more money on social
services they thought would reduce crime.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:00.920
DOES THIS SOUND FAMILIAR?

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:07.840
Guerry proclaimed, “We are forced to recognize
that the facts of the moral order are subject,

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.260
like those of the physical order, to invariable
laws.”

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:17.959
The truth is that crime statistics are HIGHLY
variable.

00:03:17.959 --> 00:03:19.069
Highly.

