00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:46.960
The first problem is uniformity -- in police
forces, in data collection, and in laws.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:47.960
Cool.

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:48.960
WHAT DOES THAT MEAN?

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:49.960
Here’s an example:

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:58.330
Let’s say the rate of assault annually is
1,655 per 100,000 in Scotland, 23 in India,

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:00.880
and 291 in Estonia.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:06.970
Is a Scottish guy just 70 times more likely
to punch you in the face than an Indian?

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:09.180
Are Estonians relatively well-behaved?

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:12.520
Let’s analyze the data.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:18.639
Scotland has a well publicized alcoholism
epidemic and alcohol is a frequent component

00:05:18.639 --> 00:05:20.180
of assaults.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:26.699
Scotland consumes 9.4 liters of pure alcohol
per adult every year, and India consumes just

00:05:26.699 --> 00:05:29.080
5.7 liters.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:34.800
So it would make sense that Scotland would
have a lot more assaults -- which was a major

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:39.020
argument by the teetotaler movement and Prohibition.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:46.620
But Estonia consumes 11.6 liters per adult
annually, and their assault numbers are way

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:52.389
lower than Scotland’s, so… it’s more
complex than this.

00:05:52.389 --> 00:05:54.120
Maybe it’s more police.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:58.290
Scotland has 318 per 100,000 residents.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:05.350
India has just 152 -- so less policing might
mean fewer reported crimes.

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:10.600
Is it just easier to get caught and held responsible
in Scotland?

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:17.810
Estonia has 336 police per 100k, which is
a little more than Scotland.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:24.560
So… does that additional 5.6% of police
presence make a huge difference in deterring

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:25.919
assaults?

00:06:25.919 --> 00:06:27.530
I doubt it.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:30.800
This has to be a question of laws.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:36.830
Maybe Scotland’s assault laws are so loosely
defined that their rate is artificially inflated.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:38.900
Well… no.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:44.110
Scottish law considers assault to be an act
that causes bodily harm or leads one to think

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:48.840
bodily harm is imminent, which is pretty sensible.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:55.560
Are India’s definitions of assault so narrowly-defined
and severely restricted that hardly anything

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:56.560
qualifies?

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:03.350
It’s actually the complete opposite -- Section
351 of The Indian Penal Code has such a broad

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:09.160
definition that it includes shaking your fist
in a threatening manner!

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:13.150
So, what’s the answer here?

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:16.699
Do the Scottish just report everything to
the police?

00:07:16.699 --> 00:07:23.410
Do Indians handle fist-shaking problems themselves,
so police never even know about the crimes?

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:30.620
Maybe Estonia is obsessive about crime tracking
and the Indians are a decade behind on their

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:31.620
paperwork.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:32.979
Who knows?!

00:07:32.979 --> 00:07:40.770
This is the problem with “moral statistics”
-- what looks like an apples to apples comparison

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:44.940
can be apples to aardvarks.

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:48.949
The second problem is data can change over
time.

00:07:48.949 --> 00:07:52.569
In Boston, the Cambridge-Somerville Youth
Study was conducted in 1945.

00:07:52.569 --> 00:07:54.250
It went like this.

00:07:54.250 --> 00:08:00.930
253 pairs of boys were matched by age, IQ,
and a range of social factors.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:05.759
Researchers flipped a coin to see which of
the 2 would receive counseling, tutoring for

00:08:05.759 --> 00:08:08.990
school, and even a summer camp getaway.

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:16.080
The idea was that delivering all those social
supports would improve their life’s outcomes.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:19.940
The research showed it didn’t really do
anything.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:26.400
30 years later, a researcher followed up the
lives of all the boys involved in the study

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:33.810
-- and found that the ones who received counseling
committed MORE crime and had higher rates

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:36.719
of alcoholism and mental illness.

00:08:36.719 --> 00:08:39.190
Which is PRETTY WEIRD.

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:44.660
The third problem is money -- you’ve got
to show results to keep your jobs and your

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:52.200
funding -- that perverse incentive seems to
have led the LAPD to misclassify 1,200 violent

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:59.470
crimes as minor offenses in 2012-2013 to reduce
its crime rate on paper.

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:07.019
That same year, Chicago Police Department
erased murders from official crime statistics

00:09:07.019 --> 00:09:10.329
once they realized they wouldn’t be able
to solve them.

00:09:10.329 --> 00:09:15.980
In 1946, criminology professor Paul Tappan
wrote that we “study the criminals convicted

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:21.560
by the courts,” and “not the real criminal
at all” -- but an insignificant proportion

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:27.550
of non-representative and stupid unfortunates
who happened to become enmeshed in technical

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:29.750
legal difficulties.”

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:37.519
And that’s the fourth problem -- a significant
portion of crimes go unreported, and many

00:09:37.519 --> 00:09:42.079
of the major criminals go free while their
foot soldiers get caught.

00:09:42.079 --> 00:09:47.260
But even when we know a crime has happened
and who’s responsible, we’d struggle to

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:48.860
know why.

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:50.250
Is there correlation?

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:51.709
Is there causation?

00:09:51.709 --> 00:09:58.130
Does a decrease in crime reflect a new law
being effective or better policing, or is

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:04.510
it a statistical mirage caused by a regression
to the mean -- or some social factor no one

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:06.630
has even considered?

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:12.380
And what are the unintended consequences of
our interventions?

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:18.160
The fifth problem is that the media reports
the numbers it’s got -- and sometimes the

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:24.780
numbers it’s incentivized to report, which
can lead to media-generated crime waves and

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:26.510
moral panics.

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:31.250
Crime rates affect property values, where
people choose to live, what schools their

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:32.880
kids attend.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:40.160
They determine where we as a society assign
blame, and they can infect our social attitudes.

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:46.910
Our perception of crime -- sometimes too strong,
sometimes too light -- informs who we elect

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:52.120
to lead and write laws for towns, cities,
states, and nations.

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:56.940
The final problem is... you.

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:02.829
Did you ever do something bad as a kid, and
when your mom asked you, “Why did you do

00:11:02.829 --> 00:11:03.829
it?”

00:11:03.829 --> 00:11:09.250
you said, “I don’t know” -- and you
really, truly did not know?

00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:15.029
It’s the same with criminology -- there’s
such a complex relationship of psychological,

00:11:15.029 --> 00:11:21.070
biological, social, emotional, and economic
factors that even when it’s happening in

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:27.100
our own heads, we usually can’t make any
sense of it.

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:33.899
So if we can’t trust the numbers and we
can’t trust ourselves…

00:11:33.899 --> 00:11:35.920
what are we left with?

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:43.250
Knowing that we don’t know is probably a
good place to start.

00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:52.509
And as always, thanks for watching.

